Article 9 In addition to the special circumstances under which tendering may be dispensed with as provided in Article 66 of the Tendering and Bidding Law, tendering may be dispensed with under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The project requires the use of irreplaceable patents or proprietary technologies;
(2) The procuring entity is able to construct, produce, or provide the goods or services itself according to law;
(3) An investor in a franchised project that has already been selected through tendering is able to construct, produce, or provide the goods or services itself according to law;
(4) The project requires the procurement of engineering, goods, or services from the original winning bidder, failing which the construction or functional compatibility requirements would be affected;
(5) Other special circumstances as prescribed by the State.
Where a tenderer practices fraud for the purpose of applying the provisions of the preceding paragraph, such conduct shall constitute evasion of tendering as provided in Article 4 of the Tendering and Bidding Law.

Article 32 A tenderer shall not restrict or exclude potential bidders or bidders by means of unreasonable conditions.
Any of the following acts committed by a tenderer shall constitute restricting or excluding potential bidders or bidders by means of unreasonable conditions:
(1) Providing differentiated project information to potential bidders or bidders for the same tendering project;
(2) Setting qualification, technical, or commercial conditions that are incompatible with the specific characteristics and actual needs of the tendering project or unrelated to the performance of the contract;
(3) For a project that must undergo tendering according to law, using the performance or awards of a specific administrative region or specific industry as a condition for extra points or a condition for winning the bid;
(4) Adopting different qualification examination or bid evaluation standards for potential bidders or bidders;
(5) Restricting or specifying particular patents, trademarks, brands, places of origin, or suppliers;
(6) Illegally restricting the forms of ownership or organizational forms of potential bidders or bidders in a project that must undergo tendering according to law;
(7) Restricting or excluding potential bidders or bidders by other unreasonable conditions.

Article 39 Bidders are prohibited from colluding with each other in bidding.
Any of the following circumstances shall constitute collusion among bidders:
(1) Bidders consult with each other on the substantive contents of bid documents, such as bid prices;
(2) Bidders agree among themselves on the winning bidder;
(3) Bidders agree among themselves that some bidders will give up bidding or winning the bid;
(4) Bidders that are members of the same group, association, chamber of commerce, or other organization coordinate their bids in accordance with the requirements of such organization;
(5) Other joint actions taken by bidders to seek to win the bid or to exclude specific bidders.

Article 40 Any of the following circumstances shall be deemed as collusion among bidders:
(1) The bid documents of different bidders are prepared by the same unit or individual;
(2) Different bidders entrust the same unit or individual to handle bidding matters;
(3) The project management members specified in the bid documents of different bidders are the same person;
(4) The bid documents of different bidders are unusually consistent or the bid prices show regular differences;
(5) The bid documents of different bidders are intermixed;
(6) The bid bonds of different bidders are transferred from the account of the same unit or individual.

Article 41 The tenderer is prohibited from colluding with bidders.
Any of the following circumstances shall constitute collusion between the tenderer and a bidder:
(1) The tenderer opens bid documents before bid opening and discloses relevant information to other bidders;
(2) The tenderer directly or indirectly discloses the base price, members of the bid evaluation committee, or other information to bidders;
(3) The tenderer explicitly or implicitly suggests that bidders lower or raise their bid prices;
(4) The tenderer instructs a bidder to replace or modify its bid documents;
(5) The tenderer explicitly or implicitly suggests that a bidder facilitate a specific bidder's winning of the bid;
(6) Other collusive actions taken by the tenderer and a bidder to seek a specific bidder's winning of the bid.

Article 42 Bidding by using qualification or qualification certificates acquired through transfer, lease, or other means shall constitute bidding in the name of others as specified in Article 33 of the Tendering and Bidding Law.
Any of the following circumstances of a bidder shall constitute the act of practicing fraud by other means as specified in Article 33 of the Tendering and Bidding Law:
(1) Using forged or altered licenses;
(2) Providing false financial status or performance records;
(3) Providing false resumes of the project leader or key technical personnel, or false labor relationship certificates;
(4) Providing false credit status;
(5) Other fraudulent acts.

Article 51 The bid evaluation committee shall reject a bid under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The bid document is not sealed with the seal of the bidding unit and signed by the person in charge of the unit;
(2) The bidding joint venture fails to submit a joint bidding agreement;
(3) The bidder does not meet the qualification conditions specified by the State or in the tender documents;
(4) The same bidder submits two or more different bid documents or bid prices, except where the tender documents require the submission of alternative bids;
(5) The bid price is lower than the cost or higher than the maximum bid price set in the tender documents;
(6) The bid document does not respond to the substantive requirements and conditions of the tender documents;
(7) The bidder has engaged in illegal acts such as collusion in bidding, fraud, or bribery.

Article 64 If a tenderer falls under any of the following circumstances, the relevant administrative supervision department shall order it to make corrections and may impose a fine of not more than 100,000 yuan:
(1) Adopting invited tendering when open tendering should be adopted according to law;
(2) The time limits for selling, clarifying, or modifying the tender documents or pre-qualification documents, or the determined time limits for submitting pre-qualification application documents or bid documents, do not comply with the provisions of the Tendering and Bidding Law and these Regulations;
(3) Accepting units or individuals that have not passed pre-qualification to participate in bidding;
(4) Accepting bid documents that should be rejected.
If a tenderer commits any of the acts listed in subparagraphs (1), (3), or (4) of the preceding paragraph, the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible of the unit shall be given sanctions according to law.

Article 67 If bidders collude with each other in bidding or collude with the tenderer in bidding, or if a bidder bribes the tenderer or members of the bid evaluation committee to seek winning the bid, the winning of the bid shall be invalid; if the act constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law; if it does not constitute a crime, a penalty shall be imposed in accordance with Article 53 of the Tendering and Bidding Law. If the bidder does not win the bid, the amount of the fine imposed on the unit shall be calculated based on the contract amount of the tendering project in proportion as provided in the Tendering and Bidding Law.
Where a bidder commits any of the following acts, which constitutes a serious circumstance as specified in Article 53 of the Tendering and Bidding Law, the relevant administrative supervision department shall disqualify it from participating in the bidding of projects that must undergo tendering according to law for a period of 1 to 2 years:
(1) Seeking to win the bid by bribery;
(2) Colluding in bidding twice or more within 3 years;
(3) The collusive bidding act damages the lawful interests of the tenderer, other bidders, the State, the collective, or citizens, causing direct economic losses of more than 300,000 yuan;
(4) Other serious collusive bidding acts.
If a bidder commits any of the illegal acts listed in the preceding paragraph again within 3 years from the date of expiry of the penalty execution period specified in paragraph 2 of this Article, or if the collusive bidding or seeking to win the bid by bribery is especially serious, the administrative authority for industry and commerce shall revoke its business license.
Where laws or administrative regulations otherwise provide for penalties for collusive bidding on bid prices, such provisions shall prevail.

Article 68 If a bidder wins the bid by bidding in the name of others or by other fraudulent means, the winning of the bid shall be invalid; if the act constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law; if it does not constitute a crime, a penalty shall be imposed in accordance with Article 54 of the Tendering and Bidding Law. If the bidder of a project that must undergo tendering according to law does not win the bid, the amount of the fine imposed on the unit shall be calculated based on the contract amount of the tendering project in proportion as provided in the Tendering and Bidding Law.
Where a bidder commits any of the following acts, which constitutes a serious circumstance as specified in Article 54 of the Tendering and Bidding Law, the relevant administrative supervision department shall disqualify it from participating in the bidding of projects that must undergo tendering according to law for a period of 1 to 3 years:
(1) Forging or altering qualification or qualification certificates or other licenses to fraudulently obtain the winning of the bid;
(2) Using the name of others to bid twice or more within 3 years;
(3) Fraudulently obtaining the winning of the bid through fraud, causing direct economic losses of more than 300,000 yuan to the tenderer;
(4) Other serious fraudulent acts to fraudulently obtain the winning of the bid.
If a bidder commits any of the illegal acts listed in the preceding paragraph again within 3 years from the date of expiry of the penalty execution period specified in paragraph 2 of this Article, or if the fraudulent winning of the bid is especially serious, the administrative authority for industry and commerce shall revoke its business license.

Article 71 If a member of the bid evaluation committee commits any of the following acts, the relevant administrative supervision department shall order it to make corrections; if the circumstances are serious, the member shall be prohibited from participating in the bid evaluation of projects that must undergo tendering according to law for a certain period; if the circumstances are especially serious, the member's qualification as a member of the bid evaluation committee shall be revoked:
(1) Failing to withdraw when withdrawal is required;
(2) Leaving the post without permission;
(3) Failing to conduct bid evaluation in accordance with the bid evaluation standards and methods specified in the tender documents;
(4) Having private contact with bidders;
(5) Soliciting the tenderer's intention regarding the determination of the winning bidder, or accepting any requirements explicitly or implicitly raised by any unit or individual to favor or exclude specific bidders;
(6) Failing to propose a rejection opinion on a bid that should be rejected according to law;
(7) Suggesting or inducing bidders to make clarifications or explanations, or accepting clarifications or explanations proactively offered by bidders;
(8) Other acts that are not objective or impartial in the performance of duties.

Article 73 If a tenderer of a project that must undergo tendering according to law falls under any of the following circumstances, the relevant administrative supervision department shall order it to make corrections and may impose a fine of not more than 10‰ of the amount of the winning bid project; if losses are caused to others, compensation liability shall be borne according to law; sanctions shall be given to the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible of the unit according to law:
(1) Failing to issue a letter of acceptance without justifiable reasons;
(2) Failing to determine the winning bidder in accordance with the provisions;
(3) Changing the result of the winning bid without justifiable reasons after the issuance of the letter of acceptance;
(4) Failing to enter into a contract with the winning bidder without justifiable reasons;
(5) Imposing additional conditions on the winning bidder when entering into the contract.

Article 80 If a state functionary takes advantage of his position to illegally interfere in tendering and bidding activities by any means, whether direct or indirect, explicit or implicit, under any of the following circumstances, he shall be given a sanction of a demerit or a serious demerit according to law; if the circumstances are serious, he shall be given a sanction of demotion or removal from office according to law; if the circumstances are especially serious, he shall be given a sanction of dismissal according to law; if the act constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) Requesting that a project that must undergo tendering according to law not be tendered, or requesting that a project that should undergo open tendering according to law not be tendered openly;
(2) Requesting members of the bid evaluation committee or the tenderer to take the bidder designated by him as a candidate for winning the bid or the winning bidder, or illegally interfering in the bid evaluation activity by other means to affect the result of winning the bid;
(3) Illegally interfering in tendering and bidding activities by other means.
